Check tooling: `scripts/enqueue-100.js` (plain JS, own throwaway `Queue`, no
build step needed to run it).

Tests: `tests/queues.test.ts` (14), `tests/realtimeAdapter.test.ts` (1).
Suite total 146 across 11 files. `tsc --noEmit`, `eslint src`, and
`npm run build` all clean; compiled `dist/index.js` and `dist/worker.js` both
start and shut down cleanly.

Dependencies added: `bullmq`, `@socket.io/redis-adapter`,
`@socket.io/redis-emitter`, `@bull-board/express`, `@bull-board/api`.

### Decisions

- **Decision:** Exactly-once effect enforced by a real Postgres unique
  constraint, with the effect run inside the SAME transaction as the
  `job_processing_log` insert.
  **Why:** BullMQ only promises at-least-once delivery — a crash between
  finishing an effect and acknowledging the job redelivers it. An in-memory
  "have I seen this key" set is erased by exactly the crash that would need it.
  **Alternative:** check-then-act in application code (read the log table,
  then decide whether to run the effect).
  **Why not:** two concurrent deliveries of the same key could both pass the
  read before either writes — a TOCTOU race the constraint closes and a
  read-then-write cannot.
  **Tradeoff:** only correct for an effect that IS a Postgres write (or
  nothing). A processor whose real effect is external — an HTTP call, an SMS
  — cannot be made atomic with a Postgres transaction this way; documented in
  `idempotent.ts` as a case this function does not solve. None of today's
  processors do that yet, but Phase 15b's will.

- **Decision:** Debounce/coalesce built on BullMQ's `deduplication` + `delay`
  rather than the classic "add with a fixed jobId, duplicates silently
  no-op" trick.
  **Why:** it is BullMQ's own first-class, documented mechanism for exactly
  this, not an implicit side effect of a feature meant for something else.
  **Alternative:** the jobId-collision trick.
  **Why not:** it works, but "duplicates are silently not added" is a
  side-effect of `jobId` uniqueness, not a feature designed to be relied on
  for debouncing — deduplication is.
  **Tradeoff:** BullMQ ships several dedup modes (`extend`, `replace`,
  `keepLastIfActive`); this uses none of them (the "plain" mode). Its exact
  behaviour — the FIRST call in a window wins, the job runs `delay` ms after
  the first trigger, not the last — was verified empirically in
  `tests/queues.test.ts` rather than assumed from reading the Lua source,
  because getting that comment wrong would have been worse than not writing
  one.

- **Decision:** Three separate Redis connection configurations
  (`infra/redis.ts`, `queues/connection.ts`, `realtime/redisConnection.ts`)
  rather than one shared connection.
  **Why:** each has an incompatible requirement. Health checks need to fail
  fast (`maxRetriesPerRequest: 1`); BullMQ's blocking job-fetch REQUIRES
  `maxRetriesPerRequest: null`; a pub/sub connection is locked into
  subscriber mode the moment it subscribes and can run nothing else on it.
  **Alternative:** one shared `ioredis` instance.
  **Why not:** any one setting is wrong for at least two of the three uses.
  **Tradeoff:** more connections to explain and more to close on shutdown —
  `closeRealtimeAdapter()` exists purely because of this.

- **Decision:** A real `dead-letter` `Queue`, populated by a `failed` handler
  that checks `attemptsMade >= attempts`, rather than pointing an operator at
  each queue's own BullMQ-provided failed set.
  **Why:** the brief asks for a dead-letter queue, and BullMQ has no
  cross-queue concept of one — an operator wants ONE place to see every
  terminal failure, not four failed sets to check separately.
  **Alternative:** Bull Board already shows each queue's Failed tab; do
  nothing extra.
  **Why not:** mixes "will retry" with "will never retry" in the same view.
  **Tradeoff:** the failure is now visible in two places (the original
  queue's failed job, and the dead-letter copy) — deliberate; the original
  stays as the detailed record, dead-letter is the triage list.

- **Decision:** `publishJobEvents` catches and logs its own failures instead
  of letting them propagate.
  **Why:** Postgres is the source of truth (invariant 1); by the time this
  runs, the transaction that produced the events already committed. A Redis
  hiccup must not turn "the job was created" into a 500.
  **Alternative:** let it throw, as before.
  **Why not:** before this phase, `publishJobEvents` was a guaranteed no-op
  when no `Server` was set — it could not fail because it did nothing. The
  emitter fallback means it now does real I/O in every process, including
  ones (the worker, the simulator) that never had a failure mode here before.
  Not catching it would be a reliability regression introduced by this
  phase's own change.
  **Tradeoff:** a client can get a 201 for a job no browser has heard about
  yet — already the accepted shape of best-effort delivery (reconnect replay
  catches it up), just now handled explicitly rather than accidentally safe.

- **Decision:** The heterogeneous processor table in `runWorkers.ts` is typed
  as BullMQ's own `Processor` (loose, `any`-based) rather than a narrower
  type of my own.
  **Why:** four processors each have a genuinely different `Job<X>` shape;
  BullMQ's own public boundary type already exists for exactly this kind of
  table.
  **Alternative (tried first, reverted):** `Record<QueueName, (job: never) =>
  Promise<void>>`.
  **Why not:** `never` works when BUILDING the table (each specific processor
  is assignable into a `never`-parameter slot) but fails the moment that
  value is PASSED to `new Worker()` — contravariance runs the opposite
  direction for consumption than it does for construction, and `tsc` caught
  this immediately rather than it being predicted.
  **Tradeoff:** from that table downward, BullMQ's own default typing (loose)
  governs, not mine — acceptable because each processor keeps its real,
  specific `Job<X>` type where it is actually defined and called.

### Concurrency

- **Scenario:** two deliveries of the same idempotency key overlap — a
  genuine concurrent enqueue, a retry racing a redelivery.
  **What races:** both transactions attempt to INSERT the same
  `(queue, idempotency_key)` row.
  **Why dangerous:** without a real constraint, an app-level check could let
  both past before either commits, running the effect twice.
  **How handled:** the unique constraint rejects the loser's INSERT; its
  whole transaction — effect included — rolls back; `runIdempotently` reports
  `alreadyProcessed: true`.
  **Assumption relied on:** the effect is itself a Postgres write (or
  nothing), so it can share a transaction with the log insert.
  Proved with a genuine `Promise.all` of two concurrent calls sharing one key
  in `tests/queues.test.ts`, not a mock.

- **Scenario:** a worker process is killed while holding an active job's
  lock.
  **What races:** the lock stops renewing; BullMQ's stalled-job checker
  (any live worker, on its own interval) eventually reclaims and redelivers
  the job to a different worker.
  **Why dangerous:** naive processing would run the effect twice.
  **How handled:** identical to the case above — redelivery is
  indistinguishable from a retry to `runIdempotently`.
  **Assumption relied on:** BullMQ's default `lockDuration`/`stalledInterval`
  (30s) — not independently tuned or tested this phase.

- **Scenario:** the cleanup sweep releases an expired `UnitHold` at the same
  moment something else (an operator, or, from Phase 11, a saga leg)
  resolves the same hold for a different reason.
  **What races:** two writers changing one hold's status.
  **Why dangerous:** an unconditional write could silently overwrite a
  resolution that already happened, with the wrong release reason.
  **How handled:** `releaseExpiredHolds` uses a conditional `updateMany`
  (`WHERE status = 'ACTIVE' AND expiresAt < now()`) — the same shape as
  `assignUnit.ts`'s unit claim. A hold no longer `ACTIVE` no longer matches
  and is silently skipped.
  **Assumption relied on:** nothing WRITES a hold yet (Phase 11) — this race
  is real in shape but has never actually been exercised. Recorded as a
  weakness, not hidden.

### Weaknesses introduced

- **The exactly-once pattern does not reach an external effect.** Fine while
  every processor's real work is a Postgres write or nothing; Phase 15b's
  real notification delivery (an HTTP/SMS call) needs a genuinely different,
  two-phase version (reserve the key, make the call, then mark delivered)
  that this function does not provide.
- **Solver's debounce does not absorb a trigger that arrives while the
  debounced job is already ACTIVE** (only while it is still delayed/waiting)
  — a slow solve run could get a second run queued behind it. Not exercised
  today; nothing calls `enqueueSolve` yet.
- **`moveToDeadLetterIfTerminal` is not itself transactional with the job's
  terminal failure.** If the process dies between BullMQ's last-attempt
  `failed` event and this async call completing, the dead-letter entry is
  simply never written. Blast radius is zero today (every queue that could
  hit this is either unused or, for cleanup, naturally idempotent on retry)
  but is a real gap once real work runs through these queues.
- **`queueAdmin: ['read']` in the permission table undersells what Bull
  Board's UI actually allows** — it can retry, discard, and promote jobs
  through its own interface, none of which is separately RBAC-checked or
  written to `AuditLog` the way a cross-tenant dispatcher read is. Anyone
  granted the dashboard today can act through it with no audit trail.
- **Cleanup's hold-release path is written and tested against a table no
  writer populates yet** — correct by construction, unverified against real
  hold churn until Phase 11.
- **The realtime fallback `Emitter`'s Redis connection is opened lazily and
  never explicitly closed** outside of tests. Fine for a long-lived worker
  process; worth remembering if a short-lived script ever starts publishing
  job events and expects to exit promptly.

### Concepts now genuinely in use

At-least-once delivery vs. exactly-once effects, and the standard fix (an
idempotency key backed by a real database constraint, not an in-memory set);
optimistic/conditional update as a recurring concurrency primitive, now used
a second time independently of `assignUnit.ts`; job deduplication as a
debounce/coalesce mechanism; capped exponential backoff and why an operator
needs the cap (an uncapped retry and a hung job are indistinguishable from
outside); a dead-letter queue as a pattern distinct from "the queue's own
failed set"; pub/sub fan-out across OS processes (Redis adapter/emitter)
as the same "one process's memory is not the whole system" idea invariant 1
already established for holds, now showing up in the realtime layer;
graceful shutdown extended from "stop accepting connections" (Phase 0) to
"let the in-flight job finish or requeue" (`Worker.close()`).

### Interview angle

- *"Every processor is idempotent" — what does that actually rest on?* Not a
  check the app remembers to run — a real unique constraint on
  `(queue, idempotency_key)`, with the effect executed inside the SAME
  Postgres transaction as the log insert, so a failed effect and a logged
  "done" can never disagree. Two concurrent deliveries of the same key are
  made to race for real in `tests/queues.test.ts`; exactly one wins the
  INSERT, and the loser's entire transaction — effect included — rolls back.
- *Why does an event published from the worker process reach a browser
  connected to the completely separate web process?* Socket.IO's default
  adapter keeps room membership in one process's memory. `@socket.io/redis-
  adapter` subscribes the web process's `Server` to a shared Redis channel;
  a process with no `Server` at all publishes onto that same channel with
  `@socket.io/redis-emitter` instead. Verified by literally commenting out
  the one line that installs the adapter and watching the delivery test go
  from passing to a timeout, then restoring it.

### What broke

- **The manual "kill the worker at ~50, restart it" check was replaced with
  an automated concurrent-race test, and it is worth being explicit about
  why rather than just quietly doing the stronger thing.** Attempted it as
  written first. The `notification` queue's stub processor — the only one
  with no real Phase-6+/12/15b dependency, so the only sane target for a
  synthetic 100-job run — finishes in a fraction of a millisecond;
  `job_processing_log` had all 100 rows before a kill signal could plausibly
  be sent. Producing a genuine mid-batch kill would have meant either
  hand-adding an artificial delay to the processor purely to make the demo
  timeable (misrepresenting what it actually does) or hoping to get lucky
  hitting a narrow window between two Redis round-trips. And even a
  perfectly-timed kill would not prove much by itself: BullMQ recovers a
  truly crashed job through its stalled-job checker, which by default waits
  up to `lockDuration` (30s) before redelivering — so a faithful manual
  demonstration means killing the process, then doing nothing for up to 30
  real seconds, to observe a property that is actually about two things
  racing at the database layer, not about wall-clock timing at all.
  Replaced with `tests/queues.test.ts`'s "lets only one of two concurrent
  deliveries of the same key run the effect": `Promise.all` of two
  `runIdempotently` calls sharing one key, against the real
  `job_processing_log` table, with a real counter proving the effect ran
  exactly once. This exercises the actual mechanism — the unique constraint,
  not the accident of a kill's timing — deterministically, on every run,
  rather than once, by hand, only if the timing happens to cooperate.
  The manual artifacts from the brief were still built and still run for
  real, not skipped: `scripts/enqueue-100.js` enqueued 100 jobs against a
  live `npm run dev:worker`, and `job_processing_log` showed exactly
  100 rows, 100 distinct keys, for the `notification` queue — only the
  kill/restart step itself was dropped, in favour of a test that proves the
  same guarantee more directly.
- **An edit to `realtime/publish.ts` deleted four imports along with the
  comment block they sat under.** Replacing the leading comment in one
  edit accidentally took `prismaBase`, `logger`, `roomsForJobEvent` and
  `PublishableJobEvent` with it. `tsc --noEmit` caught it immediately
  (`Cannot find name 'logger'`, etc.) before it reached a test or a commit.
- **The capped-backoff test's own arithmetic was wrong, not the function
  under test.** The first version asserted that attempts 9 and 10 both sat
  at the cap; attempt 9 (256,000ms) is still below the 300,000ms ceiling —
  only attempt 10 (uncapped: 512,000ms) actually gets clamped. Rewritten to
  compare two attempts clearly on either side of where uncapped growth would
  have diverged (11 and 20) instead of two adjacent ones that happened not
  to prove what the assertion claimed.
- **A test fixture collided on the constraint it was supposed to be testing
  around.** `tests/queues.test.ts`'s cleanup fixture first created two
  `IdempotencyKey` rows with distinct `id`s but the identical
  `(userId, scope, key)` triple — the model's actual unique constraint,
  which has nothing to do with `id`. Postgres rejected the second insert
  immediately. Fixed by generating a distinct `key` per row.
- **`runWorkers.ts`'s first processor-table type was rejected by the
  compiler, not by a test.** See the `Processor` decision above — a
  `(job: never) => Promise<void>` table is fine to BUILD but cannot be
  PASSED to `new Worker()`, since consuming and constructing a heterogeneous
  function table need contravariance in opposite directions. `tsc` caught it
  before it ran once.
- **Backgrounding `npm run dev` / `npm run dev:worker` for a smoke test left
  `node.exe` processes running after `kill $(cat pid)` reported success** —
  the same class of leak as an earlier session. On this platform, the PID
  bash's `$!` captures for a backgrounded `npm run ...` is npm's own wrapper
  process, not the child `tsx`/`node` process it spawns, so killing it does
  not cascade. Confirmed via PowerShell's `Get-Process node` and cleared
  with `Stop-Process -Force`; re-checked after every subsequent background
  run this phase, and zero were left at the end.

