`eslint src`, `npm run build`, `docker compose config` all clean.

REAL INFRASTRUCTURE ACTUALLY BUILT, NOT ONLY DOCUMENTED. Pulled
`osrm/osrm-backend`, downloaded a genuine ~700MB Overpass export covering
Bengaluru, ran the full extract/partition/customize pipeline, and had
`osrm-routed` serving real queries on `localhost:5001` for the whole session.
Every check-block assertion was run against this, not asserted from
documentation:
- Option count equalled pair count; a broad-capability technician
  outscored a nearby narrow one on `coveredFaultProbability` specifically
  (not on distance).
- A real divided-highway pair — two points on Bengaluru's Outer Ring Road
  near Hebbal, on opposing one-way carriageways, found by querying Overpass
  for the actual OSM ways — measured 19m apart in a straight line and
  4.54km apart by road (243x).
- Stopping the container live and calling `travelTime()` against the real
  configured URL produced a logged connection-refused warning and a
  `{ degraded: true, method: 'HAVERSINE_FALLBACK' }` result, then real
  routing resumed after restarting it.

### Decisions

- **Decision:** an Overpass `/api/map` bounding-box export instead of a
  Geofabrik country or state extract.
  **Why:** Geofabrik does not offer a Karnataka-level cut for India, only the
  whole country (several hundred MB, a much longer preprocessing run) — a
  poor match for a project that dispatches inside one city. Overpass exports
  exactly the box asked for.
  **Alternative:** download all of India once and accept the size.
  **Why not:** far larger and slower for no benefit — every seeded zone is
  Bengaluru, and nothing outside the box is ever routed through.
  **Tradeoff:** dependent on a shared public Overpass instance's rate limits
  and query timeout rather than a stable, versioned file download — see What
  broke. `OSRM_OVERPASS_URL` is a script env override for exactly this
  reason.

- **Decision:** `DispatchOption` carries a `travelEstimateDegraded` field
  beyond the four the phase brief lists literally.
  **Why:** the same brief's routing section requires a degraded estimate to
  be "visible to the operator" (invariant 8), and there is nowhere else in
  this phase's actual output for that fact to live — Phase 9 gives dispatch
  options a database row, and this is what will land in it.
  **Alternative:** keep the type to exactly the four named fields and log the
  degraded state some other way (a side channel, a log line only).
  **Why not:** a log line is not "visible to the operator" in the sense the
  brief means it — it has to travel WITH the option a dispatcher or a later
  phase looks at, not live in a place only an engineer tailing logs would see.
  **Tradeoff:** a literal reading of the brief's type definition is no longer
  exact; documented at the field itself so nobody mistakes it for scope creep
  later.

- **Decision:** `travelTime`/`travelTimeMatrix` take the OSRM base URL as an
  explicit trailing parameter defaulting to `config.OSRM_URL`, rather than
  always reading the shared config directly.
  **Why:** lets a test point one call at an address nothing listens on
  without mutating global config state (fragile under parallel test files)
  or adding a mocking layer.
  **Alternative:** read `config.OSRM_URL` unconditionally inside the
  function; tests temporarily reassign the config object's property.
  **Why not:** global mutation in a test, even wrapped in try/finally, is the
  kind of thing that is safe only by accident of vitest's per-file isolation
  — correct today, fragile to depend on.
  **Tradeoff:** one more parameter on two public functions, always given a
  sensible default so no other call site needed to change.

- **Decision:** `coveredFaultProbability` sums over ALL twelve fault codes;
  `missingCapabilities` reuses the EXISTING coverage-mass "contending faults"
  calculation from `src/domain/capabilitySet.ts`. Two different questions,
  answered by two different scans of the same distribution.
  **Why:** "what fraction of the probability mass can this pair resolve" is
  a question about the WHOLE belief, deliberately not narrowed to the faults
  intake already stopped asking about — a generalist should score near 1.0
  even against real uncertainty. "What is this pair missing" is instead
  anchored to the requirement intake ALREADY settled on, reusing the one
  function that decision lives in rather than inventing a second threshold.
  **Alternative:** compute both from the same "contending faults" subset.
  **Why not:** would make `coveredFaultProbability` blind to a pair's ability
  to handle the LONG TAIL of the distribution, understating exactly the
  generalist-technician advantage the Check block asks to observe.
  **Tradeoff:** two capability-facing numbers on one option, each answering a
  narrower question than "is this a good pick" — genuinely two facts, not a
  precomputed verdict; Phase 7/8 read both for different reasons.

- **Decision:** an uncrewed unit produces no option at all, not a rejected
  one.
  **Why:** `DispatchOption.technicianId` is not nullable — a "pair" needs
  both halves, and a unit with no crew has no technician half to pair with.
  Rejection (Phase 7) is for candidates that exist but should not be sent;
  this one never existed as a candidate in the first place.
  **Alternative:** emit an option with `technicianId: null` or a sentinel,
  let Phase 7 reject it.
  **Why not:** manufactures a candidate for a pairing that cannot physically
  happen — nobody can crew a van with no driver — which is a different kind
  of "no" than "this driver is off shift."
  **Tradeoff:** a caller cannot distinguish "no options because nothing is
  crewed" from "no options because nothing exists" from the option list
  alone — both are simply zero. Acceptable since the snapshot itself already
  answers that question directly.

- **Decision:** `unitLocation` throws when a unit has neither a GPS position
  nor a resolvable zone, rather than defaulting to `0,0` or silently
  excluding it.
  **Why:** every unit's `zoneId` is a real foreign key and every zone a fleet
  actually uses should be in its own snapshot — reaching this branch means
  the snapshot was assembled wrong, not that the fleet has a genuinely
  unlocatable unit. `0,0` would silently claim the unit is off the coast of
  Africa and produce a wrong-looking-but-plausible travel time instead of a
  loud failure.
  **Alternative:** skip the unit silently, the same as the "crew id not in
  snapshot" integrity case.
  **Why not:** a missing crew reference is a plausible race (crewing changed
  between snapshot assembly and use); a fleet-wide zone list missing a zone
  the fleet's own units reference is a different class of bug, upstream in
  whatever assembled the snapshot, and deserves to be loud.
  **Tradeoff:** the function is not TOTAL over its input type — a
  malformed-enough snapshot can throw. Acceptable for a phase with no caller
  yet; worth revisiting once Phase 9's orchestrator is the one building
  snapshots for real and can decide how to handle it upstream.

### Concurrency

Candidate generation itself has none — a pure computation over an
already-frozen snapshot, no shared mutable state, nothing to race.

- **Scenario:** OSRM's `/table` batch answers most origins but returns `null`
  for one (a unit on a road-network fragment the extract's bbox cut off from
  the rest).
  **What races:** nothing races; this is a partial-failure shape, not a
  concurrency one — recorded here because it is the closest thing this phase
  has to "what happens when the world does not cooperate."
  **Why dangerous:** treating a `null` duration as `0` or throwing would
  either produce a wrong-looking instant travel time or fail an entire
  batch over one unroutable origin.
  **How handled:** only that one origin falls back to haversine; the rest of
  the batch keeps its real OSRM answer.
  **Assumption relied on:** the bbox extract genuinely covers everywhere a
  real unit or job in this fleet would be — true today because the seed only
  places things inside it; not re-verified against every possible future
  coordinate.

### Weaknesses introduced

- **Road-factor and speed constants are unmeasured against Bengaluru
  specifically** — 1.4x and 30km/h are common rule-of-thumb figures, stated
  as such in `config.ts`'s own comment, not calibrated against real OSRM
  output. Now that real OSRM data exists, checking the fallback against it
  is possible and has not been done.
- **No caching of travel-time queries.** Every `generateCandidates` call
  re-queries OSRM for the whole fleet even if the same job's location was
  used moments before. Fine at today's scale (nothing calls this in
  production yet); worth revisiting once Phase 9 calls it per re-plan.
- **A location outside the extract's bounding box and a temporarily
  unroutable location inside it look identical** — both come back as a
  per-origin haversine fallback with `degraded: true`, with no way to tell
  "this coordinate is nonsense" from "this coordinate is fine but OSRM
  briefly could not connect it." Acceptable because both cases get the same
  correct treatment (fail soft, flag it), but a genuinely bad coordinate
  would not currently surface as a louder signal than a transient one.
- **`unitLocation`'s throw path is untested end-to-end** — no phase yet
  builds a real snapshot from Prisma rows, so this is only exercised with
  hand-built test snapshots. The real integrity properties it assumes
  (`zoneId` is always a real FK, every zone a fleet uses is in its own
  snapshot) are schema-level guarantees, not independently verified here.
- **The `osrm` compose service has no healthcheck and no restart policy** —
  a deliberate choice (the app already tolerates its absence, and a
  healthcheck would only ever be read by a human), but it does mean a
  crashed container gives no docker-level signal beyond `docker compose ps`
  showing it stopped.
- **Real-OSRM tests skip themselves silently** if nobody has run
  `scripts/setup-osrm.sh`. A contributor who never runs it sees 8 tests
  instead of 8, minus the fact that 3 of them never actually ran — visible
  only via a `console.warn`, easy to miss in a large test run.

### Concepts now genuinely in use

Snapshot-based pure computation over externally-changing state (a frozen
picture in, a plain array out, with "purity" meaning deterministic given a
fixed snapshot and a reachable OSRM, not literally zero IO); real
road-network routing as a reproducibility technique — determinism as a
property of infrastructure choice (a local, versioned map extract) rather
than only of algorithm; OSRM's two mutually-exclusive preprocessing
pipelines (contraction hierarchies vs. multi-level Dijkstra) and why
`osrm-routed --algorithm mld` requires the partition+customize pipeline
specifically, not contract; many-to-one distance-matrix batching versus N
one-to-one calls; dependency injection of an external service's address as
the seam for testing a fallback path without touching global state;
empirical verification of a domain claim against real map data (querying
Overpass for the actual OSM ways behind "opposite carriageway of a divided
highway") rather than asserting it from what OSRM's documentation promises.

### Interview angle

- *The brief says compute travel time "respecting carriageway direction" —
  where is that logic?* There isn't any, on purpose. OSRM's routing graph is
  built directly from OpenStreetMap's real, directional road segments —
  divided highways are already two separate one-way ways in the data. Asking
  OSRM for a route from A to B is automatically direction-aware; the
  carriageway-awareness lives entirely in the DECISION to use real road
  routing instead of straight-line distance, not in any code this phase
  wrote. Verified on real data: two points 19 metres apart on opposite
  carriageways of Bengaluru's Outer Ring Road come back 4.54km apart by road.
- *How is this "pure function, zero imports from prisma or redis" honest
  about calling out to a network service?* Two things enforce it rather than
  just describing it. First, the OSRM address is an explicit parameter with
  a config default, never a hidden global reached into silently — a test can
  redirect it without touching shared state. Second, every travel-time result
  carries a `degraded` boolean that is `false` only when OSRM genuinely
  answered, so "pure" here means "a deterministic outcome for a given
  snapshot and a given reachable-or-not state of the world," and the actual
  boundary the phase cares about — no database, no queue — is enforced by a
  literal grep for `@prisma`/`ioredis`, not by banning `fetch`.

### What broke

- **Two Overpass downloads that looked complete were not, and for two
  different reasons.** The first attempt used `curl --max-time 60`, which
  silently killed a still-downloading file with exit code 0 partway through
  — `osrm-extract` did not fail until 2.7 million lines in, with "XML
  parsing error... unclosed token." The second, longer attempt downloaded a
  file that DID end with a well-formed `</osm>` closing tag — Overpass
  closes the document even after writing an in-band error — but contained a
  `<remark>runtime error: Query timed out...</remark>` instead of the actual
  requested data. Both are now understood as genuinely different failure
  modes: a truncated transfer versus a complete-but-empty response.
  `scripts/setup-osrm.sh` checks for the closing tag, which catches the
  first kind for certain and is honest that it cannot, by itself, catch the
  second — a truly complete download from a server that simply gave up
  gracefully is not something a client-side check can detect. Recorded as a
  known limitation rather than papered over.
- **The public Overpass instance rate-limited or 502'd after those two heavy
  attempts**, including on a second mirror tried as a fallback. Resolved by
  retrying the original endpoint with a longer timeout once the load had
  presumably passed — not a code fix, just patience, and it is why the
  script takes `OSRM_OVERPASS_URL` as an override rather than hard-coding
  one instance.
- **`it.skipIf()` reads its condition during vitest's synchronous
  test-collection phase, before any `beforeAll` hook has run.** The first
  version of `tests/routing.test.ts` set `osrmAvailable` inside `beforeAll`;
  every real-OSRM test skipped on every run regardless of whether OSRM was
  actually reachable, because `.skipIf(!osrmAvailable)` read the variable's
  unset default (`false`) at collection time, long before the hook that
  would have corrected it ran. Fixed by moving the reachability probe to a
  top-level `await` at module scope, which resolves before `describe` runs.
  Caught by noticing all three OSRM tests reported as skipped even with the
  container confirmed running by hand moments earlier.
- **A mutation that mutated nothing.** The first attempt at breaking
  "uncrewed units get no option" replaced `unit.currentTechnicianId` with
  `unit.currentTechnicianId ?? ''` in the map lookup — but `Map.get('')`
  still returns `undefined` when no technician has that id, so the existing
  `if (!technician) continue` guard kept working exactly as before and every
  test still passed. A mutation that changes nothing is not evidence the
  code is correct; it is evidence the mutation was not a real one. Replaced
  with a genuine change (falling back to an arbitrary technician for an
  uncrewed unit), which did turn the intended test red.

## Phase 7 — Feasibility / 2026-08-30 / Commit: 3c4f89b ("phase 7: feasibility")

### Built

`src/engine/feasibility.ts` — `checkFeasibility(options, job, snapshot, now)`
returns `{ feasible, rejected }`. All ten codes from the build plan:
`UNIT_UNAVAILABLE`, `VEHICLE_TYPE_INCOMPATIBLE`, `CAPABILITY_MISSING`,
`ACCESS_RESTRICTION` (two distinct triggers: basement level, likely-wrong
carriageway), `NO_TECHNICIAN_ON_SHIFT`, `SLA_UNREACHABLE` do real work;
`PART_UNAVAILABLE` (Phase 19), `NO_DESTINATION_AVAILABLE` /
`DESTINATION_NOT_AUTHORISED` (Phase 22), `TENANT_CONTRACT_EXCLUDES`
(Phase 20) are wired stub functions that never reject. `classify()` runs all
ten in a fixed order per option, returning the first — and only — reason it
fails. Zero imports from prisma or redis, confirmed by the phase's own grep.

`src/engine/candidates.ts` extended, not redesigned — Phase 6's own
`UnitSnapshot`/`JobForDispatch` gained the fields Phase 7 needs on the exact
same objects: `kind` and `status` on `UnitSnapshot` (plain string unions
`UnitKindSnapshot`/`UnitStatusSnapshot` mirroring Prisma's enums, not
imported from the generated client); `createdAt`, `slaResponseMinutes`,
`parkingLevel`, `onHighway` on `JobForDispatch`. `generateCandidates`'s own
logic is unchanged — it reads none of the new fields. `unitLocation` is now
exported so feasibility's carriageway check reuses the same zone-centroid
fallback rather than a second copy of it.

Tests: `tests/feasibility.test.ts` (25). Suite total 190 across 14 files.
`tsc --noEmit`, `eslint src`, `npm run build` all clean.

### Decisions

- **Decision:** each rejected option carries exactly ONE code — the first,
  most fundamental reason it fails — checked in a fixed order (unit status,
  then vehicle kind, then capability, then access, then shift, then SLA,
  then the four stubs), rather than listing every code an option happens to
  violate.
  **Why:** `{ option, code, detail }` is what the "Why this technician?"
  panel (Phase 14) shows an operator — one clear sentence, not a list of
  everything wrong with a candidate that was never going to be sent anyway.
  **Alternative:** collect every violated code per option into an array.
  **Why not:** more information that answers a question nobody asks — "list
  every reason" is a debugging view, "the reason" is an explanation.
  **Tradeoff:** the ORDER is now a real design decision with real
  consequences, not an implementation detail — see the ordering test below,
  added specifically because the first version of this decision was
  documented but not actually verified.

- **Decision:** `VEHICLE_TYPE_INCOMPATIBLE` is checked independently of
  `CAPABILITY_MISSING`, against the unit's KIND directly, rather than relying
  on the capability-code table to make a bike ineligible.
  **Why:** capability codes are per-row data (`prisma/seed/fleet.ts`
  currently keeps them consistent with kind, but nothing enforces that at the
  database level) — a bike that somehow had `FLATBED_LOADING` attached by a
  data error would still, physically, be unable to carry a car. This check
  is a fact about the vehicle CLASS, true for every unit of that kind
  regardless of what its capability row says.
  **Alternative:** trust `missingCapabilities` alone; a bike missing
  `FLATBED_LOADING` already produces `CAPABILITY_MISSING`.
  **Why not:** correct today, but silently stops being a real constraint the
  moment the capability table disagrees with the vehicle kind — a second,
  independent check is exactly the "defense in depth" the ordering choice
  above exists to prefer.
  **Tradeoff:** for a well-formed fleet the two checks overlap completely —
  `VEHICLE_TYPE_INCOMPATIBLE` never fires on data `CAPABILITY_MISSING`
  would not also have caught. The overlap is the point, not a bug.

- **Decision:** the "opposite carriageway" trigger for `ACCESS_RESTRICTION`
  is a travel-time-vs-straight-line RATIO (4x, at an assumed 40km/h direct
  baseline), not a true per-unit carriageway comparison.
  **Why:** the schema has no field recording which side of a divided highway
  a UNIT is currently on — only the JOB's `carriagewayDirection` exists
  (Phase 3). A ratio built from data that genuinely exists (the option's own
  OSRM-measured travel time, the straight-line distance) is honest about what
  can actually be known; inventing a per-unit heading field to make the check
  "precise" would be inventing data, not modelling it.
  **Alternative:** wait for real per-unit carriageway data before
  implementing this code at all — leave it a stub, like the four Phase
  19/20/22 codes.
  **Why not:** the build plan lists `ACCESS_RESTRICTION` as a code to
  implement now, not a future one, and Phase 6 already measured the exact
  empirical signature (a 243x ratio on a real divided-highway pair) this
  check is built to catch.
  **Tradeoff:** 4x is a threshold chosen to sit comfortably between normal
  urban detour ratios (under 2x, per Phase 6's own measurements) and a
  genuine carriageway crossing (24x-243x) — untested against the full range
  of real Bengaluru roads beyond the one pair Phase 6 found. A road with a
  legitimately convoluted but NOT divided-highway route could false-positive;
  none has been found doing so.

- **Decision:** `NO_TECHNICIAN_ON_SHIFT` uses a fixed
  `ASSUMED_SERVICE_DURATION_MINUTES` (45) added to travel time, rather than
  waiting for a real fault-specific duration model.
  **Why:** the shift check is meaningless without SOME estimate of how long
  the job takes beyond arriving — "on shift long enough to travel there" is
  not the same claim as "on shift long enough to finish the job," and only
  the second is what the code name promises.
  **Alternative:** check only that the technician is on shift AT ALL right
  now, ignoring whether their shift has time left to finish.
  **Why not:** weaker than the code's own name — "shift ends before the job
  could complete" is the brief's literal wording, not "shift has not ended
  yet."
  **Tradeoff:** 45 minutes is a placeholder, named and commented as one, not
  a measured figure — a future duration model (keyed on fault type, at
  minimum) replaces it without changing the shape of this check at all.

### Concurrency

None of its own — `checkFeasibility` is a pure computation over an
already-frozen snapshot and a `now` passed in explicitly, not read from the
system clock, so a seeded replay of the same snapshot and instant always
produces the same rejections. The same "state can change under you" caveat
from Phase 6 applies unchanged: the snapshot is a picture at one moment, and
nothing here re-checks the world after it was taken.

### Weaknesses introduced

- **The carriageway-detour ratio is a heuristic, not a fact.** 4x is
  reasoned from exactly one measured real-world pair (Phase 6's Outer Ring
  Road crossing) and Phase 6's general observation about ordinary urban
  detour ratios — not from a survey of Bengaluru's road network. A
  legitimately slow but non-divided route could in principle cross the
  threshold; none has been observed to.
- **`ASSUMED_SERVICE_DURATION_MINUTES` is one flat number for every fault.**
  A tyre change and an EV high-voltage fault do not take the same time on
  site, and treating them as if they did makes the shift check equally wrong
  in both directions — too permissive for a long job, too strict for a
  quick one. Named as a placeholder; no fault-specific model exists to
  replace it with yet.
- **The four stub checks share one weakness with the whole file: nothing
  calls `checkFeasibility` in production.** Every property here is proved
  against hand-built snapshots in tests, not against a real Phase 9
  orchestrator wiring a live database snapshot through it. The snapshot
  integrity assumption (`classify` throws if an option's pair is not in the
  snapshot) is untested against a genuine race between assembling a snapshot
  and using it.
- **`isBasementLevel` matches on a single regular expression** (`/^b/i`)
  tied to the simulator's own `B1`/`B2`/`B3` format
  (`src/sim/scenarios.ts`) — a real intake payload with a differently
  formatted parking level (`"Basement 2"`, `"-2"`) would not be recognised as
  a basement at all, silently passing a flatbed through instead of rejecting
  it. Nothing currently enforces the simulator's format as the only one
  intake can produce.

### Concepts now genuinely in use

Hard constraints as filters versus penalties, and the specific failure mode a
penalty-shaped constraint has that a real filter does not (outvoted by
enough other terms, eventually) — the concrete version of invariant 3 rather
than the abstract statement of it; defense in depth via two independent
checks that overlap on well-formed data but diverge on malformed data
(vehicle kind versus capability code); a heuristic threshold reasoned from a
single measured empirical data point rather than either a guess or a full
survey, and documented as exactly that; rejection-as-a-first-class-output —
data a UI renders, not a side channel a log line would bury.

### Interview angle

- *Why does an EV job with no certified technician not just get a very high
  cost instead of being removed?* Because a cost is a number other numbers
  can outweigh — invariant 3's whole argument. Give safety-relevant
  constraints enough SLA pressure, enough zone scarcity, enough of a bad day,
  and some combination of the OTHER cost terms eventually beats even a very
  large penalty, and the system dispatches an uncertified technician to a
  live high-voltage fault. `checkFeasibility` instead REMOVES the option —
  `feasible` never contains it, so there is nothing left for a cost function
  to accidentally out-argue. Directly tested: an EV fault with a missing
  `EV_HV_CERTIFICATION` produces `CAPABILITY_MISSING` in `rejected`, an empty
  `feasible`, and the exact same option object back, unmodified — not a
  surviving option with a number attached to it.
- *How do you decide which single code a candidate that fails several checks
  gets?* A fixed order, checked once per option, first match wins — physical
  facts about the vehicle before capability gaps before access before
  timing. This is a real design decision with a real failure mode if
  swallowed by refactoring: the first version of this file documented the
  ordering but had no test that could tell the difference between the
  current order and a shuffled one, because the only test for
  `VEHICLE_TYPE_INCOMPATIBLE` used a pair that could not also fail
  `CAPABILITY_MISSING`. Caught by hand — see What broke — and fixed with a
  test built specifically to fail both at once.

### What broke

- **A comment explaining why Prisma is not imported here failed the phase's
  own check for exactly that.** `Select-String -Path src/engine/*.ts
  -Pattern "@prisma|ioredis"` is a literal text search over the whole file,
  not a check on import statements — a WHY-comment describing "not imported
  from `'@prisma/client'`" contains the substring being searched for and
  matched it, even though nothing was actually imported. Reworded to
  describe the same fact ("the generated Prisma client") without the literal
  string. Worth remembering for every future file this check applies to:
  the grep does not know the difference between code and prose.
- **An ordering decision was documented but not actually tested, and a
  mutation proved it.** The first `VEHICLE_TYPE_INCOMPATIBLE` test used
  `missingCapabilities: []` to isolate the check from `CAPABILITY_MISSING` —
  correct for proving the vehicle-kind check works on its own, but it meant
  no test could tell "vehicle-kind checked first" apart from "capability
  checked first," because only one of the two checks could ever fire on that
  fixture. Reordering the two checks in the source changed nothing in the
  test run — 24/24 still passed. Caught only by deliberately asking "does
  this specific mutation break anything," not by the mutation itself looking
  suspicious. Fixed by adding a pair that fails both checks simultaneously
  and asserting which code wins; re-running the same reordering mutation
  against the new test now fails it correctly.
- Every other planned mutation (unit-availability, basement, carriageway
  ratio, shift, SLA, a stub firing, swallowing the snapshot-integrity error)
  caught its corresponding test on the first attempt — no further surprises
  there.

## Phase 8 — Cost function / 2026-08-30 / Commit: (uncommitted)

### Built

`src/engine/cost.ts` — the five-term weighted sum, and the terms as
individually exported functions so each is testable on its own:
`expectedTimeToResolutionMinutes`, `capabilityShortfallRisk`, `slaRisk`,
`operationalCostPaise`, and `scoreOption` composing them into a
`CostBreakdown`. Three named weight vectors in `WEIGHT_VECTORS`:
`FASTEST_RESOLUTION`, `LOWEST_COST`, `BALANCED`. Zero imports from prisma or
redis, and — per this phase's own check — the file contains none of
`fairness|gini|earnings|safety`.

`src/engine/durations.ts` — the per-fault duration model the expected-time
term is computed from. `diagnoseMinutes(fault)` and `resolveMinutes(fault)`
for all twelve fault codes, plus `escalationMinutes(fault)` composed from
`REDISPATCH_COORDINATION_MINUTES` (12) + `SECOND_DISPATCH_TRAVEL_MINUTES`
(25) + `resolveMinutes(fault)`.

`src/engine/ranking.ts` — `rankOptions`, `comparatorFor`, and the two
comparators `byCostThenArrival` / `byArrivalThenCost`. This is where
invariant 4's lexicographic ordering lives; see Decisions for why it is not
in cost.ts.

Extended from earlier phases rather than duplicated:
- `src/infra/routing.ts` — `TravelTimeEstimate` gains `kilometres`. OSRM
  returns distance in the same `/route` response and via
  `annotations=duration,distance` on `/table`, so this costs no extra round
  trip; the haversine fallback fills it from the same road-factor figure it
  already derives minutes from.
- `src/engine/candidates.ts` — `DispatchOption` gains `estimatedTravelKm`
  (so `travelDistance` is a measured road distance, not a mislabelled
  duration) and `resolvableFaults` (so the duration model can ask WHICH
  faults, not just how much probability mass). `JobForDispatch` gains
  `safetyLevel`, read only by ranking.ts.

Tests: `tests/cost.test.ts` (24). Suite total 214 across 15 files.
`tsc --noEmit`, `eslint src`, `npm run build` all clean.

Measured on the build plan's own three named checks, printed rather than only
asserted:
- 2km jump-start-only unit: 48.50 min expected resolution. 7km
  broad-capability unit: 44.00 min. Further away, fixed sooner.
- NORMAL orders `far-broad` (44.2) then `near-narrow` (53.9); HIGH_RISK
  reverses it on identical cost totals.
- FASTEST_RESOLUTION and BALANCED pick `far-broad`; LOWEST_COST picks
  `near-narrow`.

### Decisions

- **Decision:** the invariant-4 lexicographic comparator lives in
  `src/engine/ranking.ts`, not in `cost.ts`.
  **Why:** the build plan's own check greps cost.ts for the string `safety`
  and requires no match. That check cannot be satisfied by a file that reads
  `job.safetyLevel`. Rather than working around the grep, the grep is
  correctly detecting a design fact: a comparator that WRAPS a ranking is not
  part of the thing being wrapped, and putting it in the cost file would put
  it exactly where it must never be — inside the weighted sum's own module,
  one refactor away from becoming a term.
  **Alternative:** keep everything in cost.ts and reach `safetyLevel`
  indirectly (a boolean parameter, a differently-named field) to dodge the
  grep.
  **Why not:** that is defeating the check rather than passing it, and it
  would leave the comparator sitting next to five weighted terms with nothing
  structural stopping a future edit from making it a sixth.
  **Tradeoff:** two files instead of one, and a caller must remember that
  `scoreOption` alone does not order anything — `rankOptions` is the entry
  point that produces a decision.

- **Decision:** `escalationMinutes` is composed from three named parts, and
  varies per fault through the third of them.
  **Why:** the build plan is explicit that escalation must model a real
  second dispatch — wait for a new unit, its travel, its resolve time — and
  not be a flat penalty. The variation is what makes the term mean anything:
  a mis-dispatch to a tyre change and a mis-dispatch to a high-voltage fault
  are not the same mistake, and a single constant says they are.
  **Alternative:** one `MIS_DISPATCH_PENALTY_MINUTES` constant.
  **Why not:** it would make `expectedTimeToResolution` insensitive to which
  fault was mis-diagnosed, which is most of what the term exists to express.
  **Tradeoff:** `SECOND_DISPATCH_TRAVEL_MINUTES` (25) is a single modelled
  figure, because the second unit is by definition not chosen yet — there is
  no origin to route from. So escalation is per-fault in its repair time but
  not in its travel time. A scarcity-aware version (a rarer capability means
  a further replacement) is computable from the snapshot and is not done.

- **Decision:** every term keeps its own natural unit — minutes, kilometres,
  two bare probabilities, paise — and the weights carry the unit conversion
  as well as the preference.
  **Why:** normalising every term to [0,1] first would need a scale for each
  (what is the maximum plausible resolution time?), and every one of those
  scales would be another invented constant with no measurement behind it.
  Keeping raw units means `CostBreakdown` can show a dispatcher "44 minutes"
  and "7 km", not "0.62" and "0.35".
  **Alternative:** normalise each term to a 0-1 range before weighting.
  **Why not:** more unmeasured constants, and a less explainable breakdown.
  **Tradeoff:** the weight numbers look wildly inconsistent at a glance —
  1.0 next to 0.0004 — and are only comparable once you know the term's
  magnitude. Documented at the weights themselves, but it is a real
  readability cost.

- **Decision:** `capabilityShortfallRisk` recomputes the shortfall by summing
  over the fault codes rather than returning `1 - coveredFaultProbability`.
  **Why:** the identity holds only while the distribution sums to exactly 1.
  A distribution that has drifted — a bug upstream, a hand-built test fixture
  — should produce a visibly odd risk number rather than a silently plausible
  one derived from an assumption that no longer holds.
  **Alternative:** the one-line subtraction.
  **Why not:** it hides the drift instead of exposing it.
  **Tradeoff:** a second loop over twelve faults per option, and two places
  that could disagree if one is edited without the other.

- **Decision:** `slaRisk` measures how much of the REMAINING window an
  arrival consumes, not whether it breaches.
  **Why:** feasibility (Phase 7) already rejects the options that would
  breach, with `SLA_UNREACHABLE`. Everything reaching the cost function fits
  the window, so "would it breach" is a constant `false` and carries no
  information. What is left to express is that fitting with thirty seconds to
  spare is worse than fitting with twenty minutes to spare.
  **Alternative:** a breach probability, or expected minutes of overrun.
  **Why not:** both are zero for every option the cost function ever sees.
  **Tradeoff:** the term is a proxy for schedule pressure rather than a
  probability of anything, despite being typed `'probability'` as a unit
  label. The label is about its 0-1 range, not about it being a real
  likelihood.

### Concurrency

None. `scoreOption` and `rankOptions` are pure functions over an already
frozen snapshot and an explicitly passed `now`, with no shared mutable state
and no I/O — the same shape as candidates.ts and feasibility.ts, for the same
reason: a seeded replay must produce identical rankings, and anything reading
a clock or a database from inside the ranking would break that. `rankOptions`
does not mutate the array it is given (asserted in a test), so a caller
holding the feasible list from Phase 7 still has it in its original order
afterwards.

### Weaknesses introduced

- **Every duration in `durations.ts` is a plausible figure, not a measured
  one.** Twenty-four numbers (diagnose and resolve for twelve faults) plus
  the two escalation constants, all hand-set. They are internally consistent
  and ordered sensibly, but no historical job data exists to fit them
  against, and the expected-time term is only as good as they are.
- **`SECOND_DISPATCH_TRAVEL_MINUTES` is one flat number** even though the
  whole point of the escalation model is that a flat number is inadequate.
  The travel component of a re-dispatch does not vary by fault or by how
  scarce the needed capability is, though both should affect it — a rarer
  capability genuinely means a further replacement unit.
- **The weight vectors are unvalidated.** They produce the three orderings
  the build plan's checks describe, which is evidence they are not
  nonsensical, but not evidence they are good. Nothing has compared them
  against each other on a realistic run — that is Phase 10's job, and until
  then "BALANCED" is a name, not a measured balance point.
- **`FUEL_AND_WEAR_PAISE_PER_KM` and `LABOUR_PAISE_PER_MINUTE` are invented.**
  Rs 12/km and Rs 300/hour are plausible Indian service-fleet figures with no
  source behind them; `operationalCost` is therefore directionally right and
  numerically arbitrary.
- **Nothing calls `rankOptions` in production.** The whole pipeline —
  candidates, feasibility, cost, ranking — is still only assembled inside
  tests. Phase 9 wires it into a real solver run, and only then does any of
  this touch a real job.
- **`slaRisk`'s `'probability'` unit label is a range description, not a
  claim about likelihood** (see Decisions). A UI rendering it as "23% chance
  of breach" would be wrong.

### Concepts now genuinely in use

Expected value over a probability distribution as a decision input rather
than a summary statistic — the fault distribution intake produced is
consumed here as a weighting over outcomes, not collapsed to its mode;
lexicographic ordering as a strictly stronger guarantee than a large weighted
penalty, and the specific reason the two are not interchangeable no matter
how large the weight; a structured, unit-carrying result object as an
explainability requirement rather than a convenience; the distinction between
a hard filter (Phase 7, removes an option) and a soft cost (this phase,
orders what survives) as two different mechanisms for two different kinds of
"worse".

### Interview angle

- *Why does a 7km unit beat a 2km one?* Because "nearest" answers the wrong
  question. With a genuinely uncertain diagnosis — half flat battery, half
  failed battery — the 2km unit can only jump-start, so half the probability
  mass ends in a second dispatch: coordination wait, a replacement unit's
  travel, and only then the repair. Expected time to resolution is 48.5
  minutes for the near unit against 44.0 for the 7km one that can handle
  both. The far unit arrives later and finishes sooner, and the cost function
  is built to see that because it sums over the whole distribution rather
  than optimising arrival.
- *Why is safety not just a very large weight?* Because a weight can always
  be outvoted — that is what a weighted sum means. The comparator was
  deliberately mutated into exactly that (a large arrival penalty added to
  cost) to find out whether the tests could tell the difference, and every
  one of them passed. See What broke: the failure mode is not theoretical,
  and it is invisible until a scenario exists where cost and arrival
  genuinely disagree by more than the weight.

### What broke

- **THE MUTATION THAT EXPOSED A WEAK INVARIANT-4 TEST.** This is the most
  useful thing found in the phase and is worth the detail.

  **The mutation.** `byArrivalThenCost` in ranking.ts — the comparator a
  HIGH_RISK job uses — was replaced with a weighted sum that adds a large
  arrival penalty to the cost total instead of comparing lexicographically:

      const scoreA = a.cost.total + a.option.estimatedTravelMinutes * 50;
      const scoreB = b.cost.total + b.option.estimatedTravelMinutes * 50;
      return scoreA - scoreB;

  This is precisely the shortcut invariant 4 exists to forbid, one level up
  from invariant 3's version of the same argument: a penalty large enough to
  behave like a constraint is a constraint with a latent bug.

  **All 23 tests still passed.** Including the two written specifically for
  invariant 4 — "flipping the job to HIGH_RISK reverses the ranking" and "a
  HIGH_RISK job orders by arrival even when cost strongly disagrees".

  **Why they could not catch it.** Every HIGH_RISK scenario in the file used
  the same two options: a unit 6 minutes away and one 20 minutes away. A
  14-minute arrival gap multiplied by a weight of 50 is a 700-point swing,
  and the cost totals in that scenario differ by about 10. The penalty
  therefore agreed with the lexicographic answer in every case tested — not
  because the two mechanisms are equivalent, but because the arrival gap was
  wide enough that no realistic cost difference could overcome it. The tests
  were asserting the right OUTCOME while being blind to the MECHANISM
  producing it. The second test's own name — "even when cost strongly
  disagrees" — was measuring a disagreement of 10 points against a swing of
  700, which is not strong disagreement at all.

  **What the new test does differently.** It inverts both magnitudes. The
  arrival gap is made deliberately tiny — 10 minutes versus 10.5, thirty
  seconds — while the cost gap is made enormous: the faster unit cannot
  resolve the job's EV fault at all (so it carries a full escalation and a
  capability shortfall of 1.0), and the slower one resolves it outright.
  Under FASTEST_RESOLUTION weights the cost difference is many times any
  plausible arrival weight. A lexicographic comparator picks the unit that
  arrives thirty seconds sooner regardless; any weighted arrival term small
  enough to still be a "term" picks the cheaper one. The test also asserts
  the two gaps have OPPOSITE signs — the winner is worse on cost and better
  on arrival — which is what proves the comparison happened on arrival alone
  rather than on a blend that happened to land the same way.

  Re-running the same mutation against the new test fails it. The general
  lesson, and the reason this is recorded at length: a test that asserts the
  right answer is not the same as a test that pins the mechanism, and the
  gap between them is invisible until something deliberately breaks the
  mechanism while preserving the answer.

- **A comment nearly failed this phase's own grep, for the second phase
  running.** cost.ts must not contain `fairness|gini|earnings|safety`, and
  the natural way to write the "what is deliberately not in this file"
  header comment uses at least two of those words. Phase 7 hit the identical
  trap with `@prisma` appearing in a comment explaining why Prisma was not
  imported. Avoided this time by checking earlier rather than later — the
  grep was run immediately after writing the file rather than at the end of
  the phase — and by phrasing the exclusions around the words: "the
  lexicographic ordering invariant 4 requires" and "even distribution of work
  across technicians".

- Every other mutation caught its test on the first attempt: escalation
  flattened to a constant (2 failures), the comparator ignoring the job's
  risk level entirely (3), expected-time ignoring capability (8),
  shortfall risk forced to zero (5), slaRisk ignoring elapsed time (2), the
  total silently dropping a term (5), and the ranking's deterministic
  tie-break removed (1).
